{parent=ii}

Without friction, <rotational kinetic energy> is conserved:
$$
K_0=\frac12I\omega^2.
$$
The constant angular speed is $\omega=\sqrt{2K_0/I}$, so one full rotation takes
$$
\boxed{T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega}
=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{I}{2K_0}}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The work law means that friction exerts a constant opposing <torque> of magnitude $W$. Until the cone stops,
$$
I\dot\omega=-W.
$$
Its initial angular speed is $\omega_0=\sqrt{2K_0/I}$, and uniform angular deceleration gives
$$
0=\omega_0-\frac WI t.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{t=\frac{I\omega_0}{W}
=\sqrt{\frac{2K_0I}{W^2}}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

